    <h2>1. The Structural Physics: Compressive vs Flexural Tensile Strength</h2>
    <p>
      Standard gravel concrete, such as Quikrete 5000, is engineered for <strong>compressive loading</strong>—bearing downwards on footing trenches, basement columns, and sidewalk subgrades. Concrete has immense compressive strength, easily resisting 5,000 pounds per square inch of crushing force.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, a countertop slab does not fail in compression. A countertop spans across hollow cabinet boxes, cantilevers out 10 to 12 inches for bar stools, and bridges narrow 3-inch strips around undermount sink basins. When an adult leans on an overhang, the bottom face of the concrete slab experiences extreme <strong>tensile stretching</strong> (flexural tension). Standard unreinforced concrete has a flexural tensile strength equal to only 8% to 10% of its compressive strength (roughly 400 to 500 PSI). A sudden impact instantly snaps an unreinforced Quikrete slab in half.
    </p>
    <p>
      GFRC solves this problem by dispersing millions of microscopic <strong>Alkali-Resistant (AR) zirconia glass fibers</strong> throughout the cement paste. As micro-cracks attempt to form under bending loads, the fibers arrest crack propagation by bridging the tensile gap, boosting the Modulus of Rupture (MOR) to over 1,800 PSI.
    </p>

