    <h2>3. The Root Cause of Cracking: Water-Cement Ratio and Bleed Water Channels</h2>
    <p>
      Standard bag mix concrete from home centers contains coarse pea gravel. In order to pack that gravel tightly into a form and wrap around edges, DIYers are forced to pour copious amounts of water into the mixer until the concrete reaches a pourable soup consistency (slump &gt; 6 inches).
    </p>
    <p>
      Portland cement chemically requires only <strong>0.25 pounds of water per pound of cement</strong> to achieve full molecular hydration. Every ounce of water added beyond that ratio is surplus "convenience water." During curing, that free water migrates to the surface as bleed water, cutting microscopic capillary channels through the slab. When the slab dries, the evaporation creates volumetric shrinkage of up to 0.08%, resulting in severe surface spider-webbing (crazing) and upward curling at form edges.
    </p>
    <p>
      In contrast, GFRC mixes utilize advanced <strong>polycarboxylate ether superplasticizers</strong>. These synthetic polymers impart negative electrostatic charges to the cement grains, dispersing them so aggressively that a bone-dry 0.32 mix flows like pancake batter without adding a drop of surplus water.
    </p>

    <h2>4. The 4 Essential Ingredients of a Site-Batched GFRC Matrix</h2>
    <p>
      If you choose to mix your own GFRC rather than buying expensive pre-bagged boutique mixes, follow this proven commercial batch recipe per 100 lbs of dry cementitious material:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>White Portland Cement (Type I/II):</strong> 80 lbs. White cement provides a clean, neutral canvas that absorbs pigment dyes accurately.</li>
      <li><strong>Pozzolan (Metakaolin or VCAS):</strong> 20 lbs (20% replacement). Metakaolin reacts with free calcium hydroxide (lime) produced during hydration, converting it into hard calcium silicate hydrate (C-S-H), closing capillary pores and eliminating efflorescence.</li>
      <li><strong>Silica Sand (Round Grain, 30/50 Mesh):</strong> 100 lbs (1:1 ratio with cementitious binder). Round silica sand flows smoothly around fibers without cutting them.</li>
      <li><strong>Acrylic Curing Copolymer (50% Solids):</strong> 6.0 lbs liquid polymer plus 26.0 lbs water (targeting a total water-cement ratio of 0.32). The polymer forms an internal membrane that locks in moisture, eliminating the need for 7-day wet water curing.</li>
      <li><strong>AR Zirconia Glass Fibers (19mm Chipped Strand):</strong> 6.0 lbs (3% of total mix weight) blended gently into the backer mix during the final 90 seconds of mixing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Reinforcement Dynamics: Why Steel Rebar Destroys Thin Countertops</h2>
    <p>
      In foundation slabs and driveways, steel rebar is protected by at least 2.0 to 3.0 inches of concrete cover. In a 1.5-inch countertop, rebar has less than 1/2-inch of concrete coverage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because concrete is chemically porous, water and outdoor atmospheric carbon dioxide penetrate that thin skin over 2 to 4 seasons. When carbonation reaches the steel, the alkaline passive oxide layer breaks down, and the steel rusts. As iron oxidizes, its volume expands by up to 600%, generating internal bursting pressures exceeding 5,000 PSI—far exceeding concrete tensile resistance. This produces linear fracture lines running directly above the rebar cage. GFRC utilizes zirconia glass fibers that are impervious to corrosion, freeze-thaw degradation, and rust expansion.
    </p>
